>
> The "=C3=A8mbrice" is a very specific shingle, the common Italian word is
> "t=C3=A8gola" (with letters pronounced as in lojban) from the latin "t=C3=
=A8gula"
> from "t=C3=A8gere"=3Dto cover.
>
> In Italian the sound for that 'c' is t=CD=A1=CA=83<http://it.wikipedia.or=
g/wiki/Aiuto:IPA>
> (/tS/ in X-SAMPA) rather than k (/k/). Same goes for Latin since in Italy
> the Latin words are pronounced according to the ecclesiastic Latin rather
> than the classical Latin.
>
> I would go with the fu'ivla {embritce} (rembembering that the stress is o=
n
> the {ibu} ) only for that very specific type of shingle and would resort =
to
> a lujvo (probably {rudyta'o} with gloss "x1=3Dt1 is a shingle/tile coveri=
ng
> the roof of x2=3Dr2 made of x3=3Dt2") for the generic concept.
>
> mi'e mu'o la .remod.
>
>
